Part 3 Reflection

Companies choose different databases based on their needs, budget, and long-term goals.

1- A bank might choose Oracle instead of MySQL because Oracle offers stronger security, better reliability, and advanced features for handling large numbers of financial transactions.
Banks need systems that are available all the time and can protect sensitive customer data.

2- A startup might choose PostgreSQL instead of Oracle because PostgreSQL is free, open-source, and powerful.
Startups often have limited budgets and prefer a database that provides strong features without expensive licensing costs.

3- Some companies continue using SQL Server because they already use Microsoft technologies such as Windows, Azure, and .NET applications.
Using SQL Server makes integration easier and reduces training and support costs.

4- Cloud infrastructure affects database decisions because companies want systems that are easy to scale and manage.
Databases that work well with cloud platforms are often preferred because they support business growth and reduce infrastructure management.

5- Enterprise companies sometimes pay for commercial databases instead of using free alternatives because they need
professional support, security updates, guaranteed service levels, and advanced enterprise features.
For large organizations, reliability and support are often more important than saving on licensing costs.

Overall, companies do not choose databases based only on technical features.
They also consider cost, security, support, scalability, and how well the database fits their business requirements.

