Part 4 reflection

Different databases are designed for different types of organizations and systems.

1- Oracle is commonly used in large enterprises, banks, government organizations, and telecom companies because it is secure, reliable, and can handle large amounts of data.

2- SQL Server is often used by companies that already use Microsoft products such as Windows, Azure, and .NET because it integrates well with those technologies.

3- PostgreSQL is popular among startups and cloud-based applications because it is free, powerful, and highly flexible.

4- MySQL is widely used in websites, e-commerce platforms, and small to medium businesses because it is simple, fast, and cost-effective.

Although all databases store and manage data, they differ in areas such as performance, security, scalability, support, and cost.
Overall, organizations choose a database based on their business needs, budget, expected growth, and existing technology environment.
The right database depends on the requirements of the organization rather than the database itself being “better” than the others.

